Best Practices for Strong Passwords
 Passwords should be at least 16 characters long for strong security.
 Use a mix of uppercase letters, lowercase letters, numbers, and special symbols.
 Avoid dictionary words, common phrases, or personal info.
 Make each password unique to prevent compromise across accounts.
 Consider passphrases made of random words for memorability.
 Use password managers to generate, store, and manage strong, unique passwords.


Common Password Attacks
 Brute Force Attacks: Trying every possible combination systematically. Longer and more complex passwords drastically increase the time required to crack.
 Dictionary Attacks: Using precompiled lists of common passwords, dictionary words, or leaked passwords. Avoiding dictionary words mitigates this threat.
 Hybrid and Reverse brute force attacks combine methods to increase success rates.
 Attackers use automated tools to test thousands or millions of combinations quickly, so weak or reused passwords are vulnerable.

    ​

How Password Complexity Affects Security
 Increasing length of passwords exponentially increases the number of combinations, making brute force attempts take far longer.
 Using a variety of character types (lowercase, uppercase, digits, symbols) increases possible combinations, making guessing harder.
 Unpredictability (no common phrases or patterns) reduces the effectiveness of dictionary and pattern-based attacks.
 Complex passwords balance strength with memorability; overly complex but hard-to-remember passwords can lead to insecure practices such as writing them down or reusing.
 Combining complexity with practices like multi-factor authentication and regular password changes strengthens overall account security.

